<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simmern</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Simmern is a small town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunsruck">Hunsrück</a> hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. From 1410 it was the seat of the Palatinate-Simmern line of the Wittelsbachs, which in 1559 inherited the Electoral Palatinate. Its church contains the tombs of the Simmern dukes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simmern.jpg" alt="Simmern" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stan Shebs · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simmern_street_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Simmern lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunsruck">Hunsrück</a>, in a basin called the Simmerner Mulde, with the old town centre in the valley of the Simmerbach and newer districts on the surrounding heights. The Külzbach joins the Simmerbach on the western outskirts, and the 50th parallel of north latitude runs through the municipal area. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahe">Nahe</a> are each about 25 kilometres away, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> is 55 kilometres to the east. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the town lay on the army road from Bingen to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Simmern was first mentioned in 1072, though the site had been settled in Roman times. It belonged first to the counts of the Nahegau and then to the Raugraves, who were given it as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> by the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> between 1323 and 1330. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Louis the Bavarian</a> granted town rights in 1330, with the right to fortify, and the town built a double wall with towers and gates. Its weekly and yearly markets drew dealers from across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunsruck">Hunsrück</a>. Before the end of the 14th century it passed to the Wittelsbach counts palatine.</p><p>After the death of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupert-of-germany">Ruprecht III</a> the Wittelsbachs divided into several lines, among them the Palatinate-Simmern line, which kept its residence in the town. Duke Johann II ruled from 1509 to 1557, had the town's first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> shop built, promoted the arts, especially sculpture, and introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. His successor Friedrich III, called the Pious, turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> in 1563. In 1559 the Palatinate-Simmern line succeeded the extinct main line as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>.</p><p>Friedrich V, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-v-of-the-palatinate">Winter King</a>, brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> to the Palatinate, but Simmern, fortified by Karl I Ludwig, came through relatively unscathed. When the Palatine line died out in 1685, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> claimed the inheritance, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a> began in 1688. On 17 September 1689 French troops left almost all of Simmern in rubble and razed the ducal residence. Only Saint Stephen's Church, the Powder Tower and a few houses survived. The French annexed the town after the Revolution, and in 1815 it was assigned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Simmern has been part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a> since 1946. It is the district seat of the Rhein-Hunsrück-Kreis and has carried the name element <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunsruck">Hunsrück</a> since 1 June 1980. Its Powder Tower is also known as the Schinderhannes Tower, after the robber Johannes Bückler, who was held there for half a year in 1799 and escaped.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Simmern im Hunsrück” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On his death the lands were divided among his four sons, creating the electoral line and the branches of Neumarkt, Simmern-Zweibrücken and Mosbach; later extinctions repeatedly brought the electorate to collateral lines, the <b>Simmern</b> in 1559, the Catholic Neuburg in 1685 and the Sulzbach in 1742.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The 1410 division of King Rupert's lands gave his son Stephen the territories of <b>Simmern</b> and Zweibrücken, to which his marriage added part of the County of Sponheim.</blockquote><a href="/state/palatinate-simmern">Palatinate-Simmern</a></li></ul></section>
